  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="arguments">
    <title>Arguments and Parameters</title>

    <idx>arguments</idx>
    <idx>parameters</idx>

    <p>
  Let’s take another look at the <i>This Old Man</i> song and see if we can
  replace more repeated code. Each verse of the song is similar except it uses a different number and rhyme.
  If we can pull these out into variables, we can write a single function that can print any verse of the song!
  Click on the words that are different in the lines that are repeated to
  discover what variables we need to add to the functions.
</p>

<activity label="this-old-man-find-different-words">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Click on the words that are different in the repeated lines.
    </p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Look for lines that are similar except for a different number or action
      and click on those words.
    </p>
  </feedback>

  <areas>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played</area> <area correct="yes">one</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my</area> <area correct="yes">thumb</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man, he played</area> <area correct="yes">two</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("He played knick-knack on my</area> <area correct="yes">shoe</area><area correct="no">.")</area></cline>
  <cline><area correct="no">print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")</area></cline>
    <cline><area correct="no">print("This old man came rolling home.")</area></cline>
  </areas>
</activity>



<p>
  We can make functions even more powerful and more abstract by giving them
  parameters for the data that they need to do their job. A
  <term>parameter</term> is a variable listed in the definition of a function
  and can be used inside the function body. This allows values, called
  <term>arguments</term>, to be passed into the function. The argument value is copied into the 
  corresponding parameter variable. Many people use the terms "parameter" and "argument" interchangeably, but formally, 
  the parameter is the variable in the function definition and the argument is the value passed to the function when it is called. 
</p>
<p>
  For example, here is a procedure called <c>forward</c> with a parameter variable called <c>pixels</c> that we used with turtles 
  with procedure calls like <c>forward(50)</c> telling it to move forward 40 or 100 pixels. The value 40 is the argument that is passed to the parameter variable <c>pixels</c> in the procedure definition.
</p>

  <image source="_static/Unit3images/procedure-defn-call.png" alt="Parameter and Argument" width="90%"/>

 <note>
      <title>3.2.A.3</title>
      <p>
        Procedures can use parameters to receive input. A <term>parameter</term> is a variable name
        listed in the procedure definition. An <term>argument</term> is the actual value provided
        when the procedure is called.
      </p>
    </note>

<p>Going back to our song, we can make a function called <c>verse</c> that takes the number and the rhyme as parameters and uses them to 
  print any verse! The parameter variables <c>number</c> and <c>rhyme</c> will hold different values each time the function is called. </p>

<program language="python"> 
  <code> 
# This function prints a verse for a given number and rhyme. 
# @param number - the number used in the verse 
# @param rhyme - the word that rhymes with the number

def verse(number, rhyme):
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()
</code>
</program>

<p>The <c>verse</c> function can now be used to print any verse of the song. The values passed to the function become the values of the parameter variables. The main part of the program can now mostly be calls to the <c>verse</c> function. Main program code often looks like an outline for the program, calling functions to do the work. </p>

<program language="python"> 
  <code> 
verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
</code>
</program>

<p>Notice that the same function is called two times with different arguments. When <c>verse("one", "thumb")</c> is called, the 
  parameter <c>number</c> gets the value <c>"one"</c> and the parameter <c>rhyme</c> gets the value <c>"thumb"</c>. When <c>verse("two", "shoe")</c> is called, the same parameters receive different values. The function does not need to change. The 
  arguments provide the values that makes each verse different. </p>

  <image source="_static/Unit3images/procedure-verse-call.png" alt="Calling verse(number, rhyme)" width="70%"/>


<activity label="codelensSong2"> 
  <statement> 
    <p> Click on the next button below the code to step through the code. Watch the red arrow jump to the <c>verse</c> function each time 
      it is called. Pay attention to how the arguments are passed into the parameter variables. 
    </p> </statement>

<program interactive="codelens" language="python" label="codeLens3" xml:id="codelensid3"> 
  <code>
def chorus(): 
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")

def verse(number, rhyme): 
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()

verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
</code>
</program>
</activity>

<p> Let’s try adding more verses to the song. With the power of abstraction using a function with parameters, 
  it becomes very easy to add more verses to the song, just by calling the <c>verse</c> function with different arguments.
  Parameters make it more general, flexible, and reusable! 
 </p>

<activity label="ThisOldManSong2"> 
  <statement> 
    <p>Run the following code to see the song <i>This Old Man</i> using the <c>verse</c> function. 
  Add another verse using <c>"three"</c> and the rhyme <c>"knee"</c> and another verse using <c>"four"</c> and the rhyme <c>"door"</c> by
  calling the <c>verse</c> function with the two arguments. 
</p>

</statement>

<program interactive="activecode" language="python"> 
  <code> 
def chorus(): 
    print("With a knick-knack paddywhack, give a dog a bone,")
    print("This old man came rolling home.")

def verse(number, rhyme): 
    print("This old man, he played", number, ".")
    print("He played knick-knack on my", rhyme, ".")
    chorus()

verse("one", "thumb")
verse("two", "shoe")
# TODO:
# Call verse again with "three" and "knee"
# Call verse again with "four" and "door"


</code>

<tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
       
        self.assertIn("verse(\"three\",\"knee\")", code, "Call verse() with \"three\" and \"knee\"")
        self.assertIn("verse(\"four\",\"door\")", code, "Call verse() with \"four\" and \"door\"")

myTests().main()
</tests>

</program> 
</activity>

  
    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.4</title>
      <p>
        A procedure may not need parameters when it always performs the same steps with
        no changing input (for example, displaying an instructions screen or resetting a
        game to a default starting state). Procedures need parameters when they need
        input information to complete the intended task. If a procedure should work for
        many different inputs (for example, greeting different users or calculating totals
        for different numbers), parameters make it flexible and reusable because the
        program can pass different arguments each time it is called. Using parameters
        when input varies helps avoid duplicating code and keeps the program easier to
        update.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.5</title>
      <p>
        Procedures with parameters make it easier to adapt an app's behavior or output
        to different users and situations without rewriting code. A single procedure with
        parameters can generate many clear, personalized messages for different users by
        using different argument values each time it is called.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>Let's now write our own functions with parameters. Let's write a procedure or function that welcomes a user.
      In AP CSP pseudocode, we would start the procedure definition with the header <c>procedure welcome(name)</c> where the parameter variable is put inside the parentheses after the procedure name.
    </p>

   <image source="_static/Unit3images/procedure-welcome.png" alt="Parameter and Argument" width="50%"/>
 
    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.6</title>
      <p>
        A procedure is defined with a name and a list of zero or more parameters. When
        the following code runs, a procedure named <c>pName</c> is defined with zero or
        more parameters (<c>p1, p2, ...</c>). When the procedure is called, the statements
        in <c>&lt;block of statements&gt;</c> execute.
      </p>
      <pre>
procedure pName(p1, p2, ...)
{
   &lt;block of statements&gt;
}</pre>
    </note>

        <p>In AP CSP pseudocode, a procedure with parameters is defined using the following syntax:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
procedure procedureName(param1, param2, ...)
{
   &lt;block of statements&gt;
}
</code>
    </program>
 
    <p>In Python, a function with parameters is defined using the following syntax:</p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
def functionName(param1, param2, ...):
    # function body
</code>
    </program>


    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.3</title>
      <p>
        In a procedure call, the values inside the parentheses are <term>arguments</term>. When the
        call runs, each argument value is assigned to the matching parameter in the
        procedure definition. This allows the same procedure to run with different input
        values. When the following code runs, <c>a1</c> is assigned to the first parameter
        and <c>a2</c> is assigned to the second parameter before the procedure's statements
        execute.
      </p>
      <pre>
pName(a1, a2)</pre>
    </note>

    <p>Python and AP CSP pseudocode have very similar simple procedure or function call with the procedure name
      followed by the argument values inside the parentheses. The number of arguments in a call must match the number of parameters in the 
      procedure definition. Each argument value is assigned to the matching parameter in the procedure definition in order.
    </p>
    <program language="python">
      <code>
# Python and AP CSP pseudocode procedure/function call
functionName(arg1, arg2, ...)
    </code>
    </program>

    <activity label="welcome-function">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write a procedure called <c>welcome</c> that takes a parameter for the user's
          name and prints Hello using the name. Then call the procedure with your name as
          the argument.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
          # TODO: Write the welcome function using def and a parameter name

          # Function Calls
          welcome("World")
          # Call welcome with your name as the argument
          # Call welcome again with a different name as the argument

        </code>
        <tests>
from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

class myTests(TestCaseGui):
    def test1(self):
        code = self.getEditorText().replace(" ", "")
        self.assertIn("def", code, "Define the welcome function with def")
        self.assertIn("welcome(name)", code, "Define the welcome function with a parameter name")
        self.assertIn("World", self.getOutput(), "The output should include a welcome message to World")
        count = code.count("welcome(")
        self.assertLessEqual(4, count, "Call welcome() at least 2 more times")

myTests().main()
</tests>
</program>
    </activity>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.4</title>
      <p>
        A procedure can also be called with no arguments (empty parentheses) when it has
        no parameters. The number of arguments in a call must match the number of
        parameters in the procedure definition.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.5</title>
      <p>
        To determine the result of a procedure call, programmers trace the call by
        matching arguments to parameters, stepping through the procedure's statements in
        order, tracking any variable updates and outputs, and then returning to where the
        procedure was called to continue program execution.
      </p>
    </note>

    <p>When tracing through code that has procedures/functions, it is important to write down and keep track of the 
      parameter variables and their values with each function call. Remember to match the arguments with the parameter in order.
    </p>
    
  <activity label="parametersTrace">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Consider the following pseudocode for a procedure <c>song</c>.
    </p>
<program language="python">
  <code>
procedure song(food1, food2)
{
    print("I like to eat, eat, eat", food1, "and", food2, "!")
}
</code>
</program>
<p>Given the following procedure calls, what would be printed when the code is run? </p>
  <program language="python">
    <code>
song("bananas", "apples")
song("pizza", "pineapples") 
</code> </program>
 </statement>
  <choices randomize="yes">
<choice>
  <statement>
    <p><c>I like to eat, eat, eat apples and bananas!</c></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      The first procedure call is <c>song("bananas", "apples")</c>, so the first argument, <c>"bananas"</c>, is assigned to the
      <c>food1</c> parameter.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>

<choice correct="yes">
  <statement>
    <p><pre>
I like to eat, eat, eat bananas and apples!
I like to eat, eat, eat pizza and pineapples!
</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Correct! The first procedure call is <c>song("bananas", "apples")</c>, so the first argument, <c>"bananas"</c>, is assigned to the
      <c>food1</c> parameter and the second argument, <c>"apples"</c>, is assigned to the <c>food2</c> parameter. The second procedure call is <c>song("pizza", "pineapples")</c>, so the first argument, <c>"pizza"</c>, is assigned to the <c>food1</c> parameter and the second argument, <c>"pineapples"</c>, is assigned to the <c>food2</c> parameter.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
  <statement>
    <p><pre>
I like to eat, eat, eat apples and bananas!
I like to eat, eat, eat pineapples and pizzas!
</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Remember that arguments are assigned to parameters in order. The
      first argument is assigned to the first parameter variable, etc.
    </p>
  </feedback>
</choice>

<choice>
  <statement>
    <p><pre>
I like to eat, eat, eat pizzas and pineapples!
</pre></p>
  </statement>

  <feedback>
    <p>
      Both procedure calls will print a line, so there should be two lines printed. 
    </p>
    </feedback>
</choice>
  </choices>
</activity>


    </subsection>
    
    <subsection xml:id="return-values">
    <title>Return Values</title>
<idx>return values</idx>
<idx>return</idx>
       <note>
      <title>3.2.A.7</title>
      <p>
        Some procedures use a return statement to send a result back to the part of the
        program that called the procedure, which allows the returned value to be stored in
        a variable, used in an expression, or displayed as output. The return statement
        ends the procedure immediately, even if more statements appear later in the
        procedure. When the following code runs in the body of a procedure, the value of
        <c>expression</c> is returned to the place in the program where the procedure was called.
      </p>
      <pre>
return expression</pre>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.A.8</title>
      <p>
        Some procedures perform an action, such as displaying output or updating
        variables, and do not return a value. Other procedures compute a value and use
        a return statement so the result can be used elsewhere in the program.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.B.6</title>
      <p>
        If a procedure returns a value, the procedure call evaluates to that value. The
        returned value can be stored in a variable, used in an expression, or displayed
        as output. If a procedure does not return a value, the call is used to perform
        actions such as updating variable values or producing output.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="procedural-abstraction">
    <title>Procedural Abstraction</title>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.1</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction manages complexity by grouping related code into named
        procedures that can be called from other parts of a program.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.2</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction allows programmers to use a procedure by knowing what it
        does without needing to know how it works internally. Programmers often use
        built-in procedures, such as <c>print</c> or <c>random</c>, by relying on the
        procedure's name, purpose, and expected behavior while the implementation details
        stay hidden.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.3</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction helps manage complexity by breaking a large problem into
        smaller subproblems and creating procedures to solve each part.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.4</title>
      <p>
        The subdivision of a program into smaller, independent procedures or components
        is called modularity. Modularity helps programmers focus on one part at a time
        and makes programs easier to build, test, and revise.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.5</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction supports code reuse by extracting shared behavior into a
        single procedure instead of duplicating code in multiple places.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.6</title>
      <p>
        Using parameters generalizes a procedure so it can be reused with different
        inputs, allowing one procedure to handle many situations without rewriting code.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.7</title>
      <p>
        Procedural abstraction allows programmers to change a procedure's internal steps
        (for example, to improve efficiency or fix a bug) without changing the rest of
        the program. This makes larger programs easier to maintain because updates can be
        made in one place.
      </p>
    </note>

    <note>
      <title>3.2.C.8</title>
      <p>
        Using procedural abstraction improves readability because the rest of the program
        can call procedures with descriptive names, so the overall steps of the program
        are easier to understand at a glance.
      </p>
    </note>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="LOs-3-2">
    <title>Learning Objectives Review</title>

    <remark>
      <title>3.2.A</title>
      <p>
        Develop code to define procedures with and without parameters.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.2.B</title>
      <p>
        Develop code for procedure calls, and determine the result of procedure calls.
      </p>
    </remark>

    <remark>
      <title>3.2.C</title>
      <p>
        Explain how the use of procedural abstraction manages complexity in a program.
      </p>
    </remark>
  </subsection>

